 ## Overview
-Volume Ray Casting with Barycentric Interpolation and Phong Lighting is a direct volume rendering technique that casts parallel rays from a viewing plane through a 3D voxel grid, sampling data values using barycentric interpolation within unit cubes and accumulating lighting contributions [S1, S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of visualizing 3D scalar fields by projecting voxel values to a 2D view plane while simulating light absorption and reflection [S1, S3, unofficial]. Unlike standard ray tracers that spawn secondary rays at intersections, this method attenuates rays as they travel through the volume, making it computationally faster but lacking advanced features like reflections and refractions [S2, S3, unofficial]. It builds on [Volume Ray Casting](3161-volume-ray-casting.md) by specifying the interpolation scheme (barycentric/linear within cells) and the shading model (Phong) [S3, S4, unofficial].
+Volume Ray Casting with Barycentric Interpolation and Phong Lighting is a direct volume rendering technique that casts parallel rays from a viewing plane through a 3D voxel grid, sampling data values using barycentric interpolation within unit cubes and accumulating lighting contributions [S1, S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of visualizing 3D scalar fields by projecting voxel values to a 2D view plane while simulating light absorption and reflection [S1, S3, unofficial]. Unlike standard ray tracers that spawn secondary rays at intersections, this method attenuates rays as they travel through the volume, making it computationally faster but lacking advanced features like reflections and refractions [S3, unofficial]. It builds on [Volume Ray Casting](3161-volume-ray-casting.md) by specifying the interpolation scheme (barycentric/linear within cells) and the shading model (Phong) [S3, S4, unofficial].
 
